WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.158 --> 00:00:15.373
As a crowd of about 75,000 people gathered in front of the Lincoln Memorial in Washington, DC, one of the most dramatic protests for civil rights was about to begin.

2
00:00:15.374 --> 00:00:26.518
Millions of others listened to their radios with anticipation as a tall African-American woman, who had faced racial prejudice all her life, walked onto the stage.

3
00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:42.396
She wasn't the type of person who, by her own admission, liked to make a fuss, but at that moment on Sunday, April 9, 1939, she was the central figure in a demonstration of resistance against racial segregation.

4
00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:47.347
The audience members held their breath as they watched what the woman would do.

5
00:00:47.348 --> 00:00:52.952
She opened her mouth and began singing. The song was "America."

6
00:00:52.953 --> 00:01:01.554
Marian Anderson, one of America's most well-known contraltos, was born on February 27, 1897.

7
00:01:01.555 --> 00:01:07.822
She was the eldest of three girls and grew up in a poor family in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania.

8
00:01:07.823 --> 00:01:15.816
At the age of six, Marian began singing at the Union Baptist Church, and her talent was instantly recognized.

9
00:01:15.817 --> 00:01:22.141
Church members nicknamed her "The Baby Contralto" for her deep and luminous voice.

10
00:01:22.142 --> 00:01:33.572
Unable to afford music lessons for their young daughter, Anderson's parents persuaded her when she was 13 to start accepting the money she was offered when she sang at local churches.

11
00:01:33.573 --> 00:01:43.136
Anderson received five dollars for each performance, and soon she had enough money to apply for admission to a local music conservatory.

12
00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:46.681
But the young vocalist was coldly rejected.

13
00:01:46.682 --> 00:01:52.932
It wasn't because of her lack of talent; Anderson wasn't even given a chance to audition.

14
00:01:52.933 --> 00:01:56.913
It was because Marian Anderson was black.

15
00:01:56.914 --> 00:02:06.726
In her autobiography, My Lord, What a Morning, she described the mortifying experience as she stood before the admissions clerk:

16
00:02:06.727 --> 00:02:15.831
"I don't think I said a word . . . It was as if a cold, horrifying hand had been laid on me. I turned and walked out."

17
00:02:15.832 --> 00:02:21.830
In response, members of Anderson's church established a fund to raise money for her training.

18
00:02:21.831 --> 00:02:33.372
Eventually, Marian Anderson was able to study under Giuseppe Boghetti, a well-known voice teacher, and he was so captivated with her voice that he gave her free lessons for a year.

19
00:02:33.373 --> 00:02:43.763
Soon she was invited to sing at churches in the South and at black colleges, and music critics began to notice the young African-American woman's phenomenal voice.

20
00:02:43.764 --> 00:03:00.330
In 1925, Boghetti entered Marian Anderson's name, without her knowledge, in New York's Lewisohn Stadium competition; the winner would get the chance to sing with the oldest symphony orchestra in the United States—the New York Philharmonic.

21
00:03:00.331 --> 00:03:08.625
When the shy singer finally learned what her teacher had done, she was nervous and afraid, but her teacher encouraged her to try.

22
00:03:08.626 --> 00:03:19.216
On the day of the competition, Marian Anderson waited her turn as singer after singer went before the impatient judges, who held noisy clickers in their hands.

23
00:03:19.217 --> 00:03:25.270
If they didn't like a singer, they would click the humiliated performer off the stage.

24
00:03:25.271 --> 00:03:34.312
When it was finally Anderson's turn, not one judge clicked at her and she beat three hundred other singers for first place.

25
00:03:34.313 --> 00:03:45.861
Marian Anderson's fame grew, and a mere three years later, she performed a solo recital at the famous Carnegie Hall, with critics praising her powerful voice.

26
00:03:45.862 --> 00:03:54.759
Still, she lived during a time of great prejudice, and many concert bookings were unattainable because of the color of her skin.

27
00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:59.129
So the young singer performed mostly for black audiences.

28
00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:09.791
However, Anderson learned the racial situation was different in other parts of the world when she made her European debut in Berlin, Germany, in 1930.

29
00:04:09.792 --> 00:04:17.728
Word of her unique voice and talent spread throughout the continent, and she was awarded scholarships to study in Europe.

30
00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:29.535
Anderson made three highly successful tours of Europe from 1930 through 1935, performing concerts in France, Germany, Belgium, and Scandinavia.

31
00:04:29.536 --> 00:04:35.113
She was even invited to sing for royalty in England, Denmark, and Sweden.

32
00:04:35.114 --> 00:04:40.254
But due to racism, Anderson was little known in her home country.

33
00:04:40.255 --> 00:04:51.694
In between her European concert tours, Marian found it even more difficult to reconcile the racial conditions in America with the relative tolerance of other countries.

34
00:04:51.695 --> 00:04:57.748
Each time she returned home, she found the racism there as harsh as ever.

35
00:04:57.749 --> 00:05:06.788
Many concert halls had segregated seating, which forced black concertgoers to sit separately from whites, usually in the balcony.

36
00:05:06.789 --> 00:05:10.801
Other halls simply refused to book black artists.

37
00:05:10.802 --> 00:05:20.176
African-Americans couldn't stay in major hotels, and in the South, the day-to-day segregation was especially intolerable.

38
00:05:20.177 --> 00:05:35.105
In 1939 Anderson's manager tried to rent one of the most prominent venues in Washington, DC, Constitution Hall, owned by an organization called Daughters of the American Revolution (DAR).

39
00:05:35.106 --> 00:05:43.550
He was told no dates were available, but the truth was that the hall allowed only white artists to perform there.

40
00:05:43.551 --> 00:05:48.713
The situation quickly became public and caused a national outrage.

41
00:05:48.714 --> 00:06:07.243
First Lady Eleanor Roosevelt resigned her membership in the DAR to protest the organization's stance, and she helped organize, along with the National Association for the Advancement of Colored People, a free outdoor concert on the steps of the Lincoln Memorial.

42
00:06:07.244 --> 00:06:18.755
During her career, Anderson had tried to remain above the racial prejudice swirling around her, but this incident thrust her to center stage in a whole different way.

43
00:06:18.756 --> 00:06:30.505
A few weeks after the Lincoln Memorial event, Marian Anderson made history once again when she became the first African-American entertainer to perform at the White House.

44
00:06:30.506 --> 00:06:47.157
In 1955, she also became the first African-American soloist at the prestigious Metropolitan Opera, and later she was asked by the State Department to be a goodwill ambassador for a 12-nation concert tour of Southeast Asia.

45
00:06:47.158 --> 00:06:55.325
The television network CBS decided to accompany Anderson on her tour for a program called See it Now.

46
00:06:55.326 --> 00:07:10.810
On September 4, 1957, just before Anderson embarked on a tour of Korea, the governor of Arkansas ordered soldiers to prevent nine black students from entering Little Rock High School, an all-white school.

47
00:07:10.811 --> 00:07:23.576
The political and racial situation in the United States caused an uproar around the world, and Anderson was peppered with questions from the press about her opinion of segregation in America.

48
00:07:23.577 --> 00:07:44.829
She was demure, saying the trouble in Little Rock "was a very unfortunate thing," but in the concert program for her Asian audiences she insisted on including a special song—"You've Got to be Carefully Taught"—about how hate and prejudice are passed down from one generation to another.

49
00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:57.676
When CBS broadcast the show, which they called "The Lady From Philadelphia," on December 30, 1957, many television stations in the South refused to air it.

50
00:07:57.677 --> 00:08:05.772
Her beautiful voice soared above any obstacles that were placed in front of her and helped her to achieve her dream.

51
00:08:05.773 --> 00:08:17.109
In 1958, Marian Anderson was appointed a delegate to the United Nations, and in 1963, she was awarded the Presidential Medal of Freedom.

52
00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:26.412
On April 8, 1993, at the age of 96, Marian Anderson died at home after a long illness.

53
00:08:26.413 --> 00:08:34.254
Her family remembered her saying "No fuss" whenever anyone raised the subject of a memorial service after her death.

54
00:08:34.255 --> 00:08:49.104
So in June, two hundred people crowded into Union Baptist Church, the place where she started her singing career, to listen to the voices of her friends and family and, last of all, the recorded voice that had touched them all.
